Short thought:

If you want to do what's best for Nethack, then yes, take your new
engine and bolt Nethack into it, along with all your changes.

If you want to do what's best for Ray, then write your own roguelike and
steal all the "good" bits from Nethack -- it's a lot easier and more
productive to design an AC system from scratch, for example, than to
bolt it onto a Nethack-like.  If you make a good one, you'll get a pile
of support, and I can say that the Crawl Dev Team and I agree on this:
we both want to see as many different roguelikes competing for attention
as possible.  Competition drives innovation, even with no money on it.

It really comes down to how altruistic you're feeling these days.  I can
say that Spork's support from the community was reasonable, but was very
closely tied to whether people thought it was a "live" fork.  Since it's
been added to NAO's menu, the support is far more significant; there are
a lot of games being played of it, far more than on my server.  It's 
remarkable how inertia can drive a community, since NAO is running from
the same svn checkout SHC is.  :)

